Done — clean compile under `-Xlint:all`, all 1072 checks pass.

## What changed

`Handle<T>` is now a **public interface** exposing only the contract:

```java
public interface Handle<T> {
    T value();
    boolean isActive();
}
```

The mutable state (`owner`, `value`, `index`) moved into a **private `HandleImpl<T> implements Handle<T>`**, which is the only implementation. Callers see just the interface; they can't reach or depend on the internal fields.

**Internals** now work with `HandleImpl<T>` throughout (`handleAt`, `place`, `swap`, `removeAt`, `insertKey`, the collection constructor). `insertKey` still returns `Handle<T>` — the concrete type upcasts automatically.

**The one substantive consequence:** an interface can be implemented by anyone, so `checkHandle` now has to guard against a foreign object that merely *implements* `Handle`. It does an `instanceof HandleImpl<?>` pattern check (rejecting outsiders) *and* the existing `owner == this` check (rejecting handles from another heap), returning the validated `HandleImpl<T>` for the caller to use:

```java
if (!(handle instanceof HandleImpl<?> impl) || impl.owner != this) {
    throw new IllegalArgumentException("handle was not created by this heap");
}
```

I added `testForeignImplementationRejected` — it constructs an anonymous `Handle` implementation and confirms `delete` rejects it with `IllegalArgumentException` — and tightened the `@throws` docs (which previously said "belongs to another heap"; now "was not created by this heap" covers both rejection cases).

**Honest trade-off note:** for a self-contained data structure like this, interface-vs-final-class is a fairly lateral call — the interface buys cleaner encapsulation and room for alternate implementations, at the cost of a downcast plus the foreign-implementation guard on every handle operation. It's a reasonable "program to an interface" choice and it's now implemented cleanly and fully tested, but I wouldn't claim it's a major correctness or performance win over the previous concrete-class version.

